sun20i regression suite (9a-0)
==============================

Build (on the VM, in this directory):

	mk tests	clean rebuild of every test, new STAMP, into stage/
	mk refs		regenerate ref/cc-*.ref with 6c (amd64) - only after
			changing a generator, then review the diff
	mk clean

Then rebuild the kernel (stage/ is packed by sun20i.bootfs.proto) and, on
the board:

	testall [area ...]	areas: cc k lc
	negall			controls: checks that testall itself fails what it should

Layout
	neg/	deliberate-failure controls for testall (see negall)
	cc/	compiler: generated int/fp differential tests (vs 6c refs),
		nan, cast, fpconst, assign
	k/	kernel: FP state across switch / fork+exec / many procs
	lc/	libc: mem* routines, atomics
	gen/	convgen.py, fpgen.py -> cc/int.c, cc/fp.c (host, regen.sh)
	ref/	6c outputs for the generated tests
	stage/	what gets packed (rc/bin/testall, rc/test/STAMP, rc/test/<area>/*)

Protocol: a test prints "build <stamp>" first, "FAIL ..." lines, and "ok"
last.  Generated tests print data after the build line and are compared with
<test>.ref instead.  testall fails a test whose build line is not the
current STAMP (stale binary), that prints FAIL, that has no verdict or
differs from its ref, or that runs longer than 300 s.

Controls: area neg holds deliberate failures (stale stamp, FAIL line, no
verdict, ref mismatch, hang).  `testall` skips it; `negall` runs it and
checks every control was reported correctly.  Run negall after any change
to testall or test.h.  testlimit=N overrides the 300 s limit.

Adding a test: write area/name.c including "../test.h", add name to TESTS
in area/manifest (and REFS if it has a reference), mk tests.

What this cannot catch: a stale libc.a or compiler.  The stamp proves the
test was rebuilt now, not that libc was reinstalled first - run
`mk install` in /sys/src/libc before `mk tests` after any libc change.
